{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially from ~day 8 onward. Apply a downward level shift (larger for longer horizons).",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: in selloffs the model reacts too slowly and keeps predicting stabilization/rebound; realized path continues drifting lower. Increase downside momentum sensitivity when the last 2–3 weeks show a clear downtrend.",
    "Overshoot on rebounds: after short-term upticks in history, the model extrapolates a stronger/longer rebound than occurs. Damp positive day-to-day changes (cap rebound slope) when the broader 20–30d trend is negative.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: error magnitude increases with forecast horizon (front week smaller, weeks 2–4 much larger). Use a horizon ramp for correction (e.g., small adjustment days 1–5, medium days 6–15, large days 16–30).",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration: the model mean-reverts toward a level that is too high (anchored to earlier higher prices). Re-anchor mean reversion to the most recent 10–15d average (or last price) rather than the 30d average in downtrends."
  ]
}
